    ipo:hasLearningObjective pg:CO1,
        pg:CO2,
        pg:CO3,
        pg:CO4,
        pg:CO5,
        pg:CO6 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09/document> ;
    skos:definition "The Stage 3 document of chapter 9 is the text this page renders, section for section."@en .

pg:TopMenu a ipo:PrimaryNavigation,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Main menu"@en ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09/document> ;
    skos:definition "The main menu is permanently visible at the top and lets the reader reach every top-level area without scrolling or opening anything first."@en .

pg:Q10_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "In 2018 Matt Corallo proposed an exception to the descendant limits for protocols such as Lightning, in which two parties sign transactions long before they are broadcast: one extra child of the transaction may be added even ..."@en .

pg:Q11_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The carve-out was a patch on top of the descendant limits, and when release 31.0 replaced those limits by cluster limits, the patch had nothing to attach to."@en .

pg:Q12_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The block subsidy is the amount of new bitcoin that the miner of a block may create in its coinbase transaction; it started at 50 bitcoins and is cut in half every 210,000 blocks, which the book describes in its chapter on ..."@en .

pg:Q13_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The book says in its section on fee sniping that the attack is not very lucrative today because the block subsidy is much higher than the total fees per block, and that at some point in the future fees will be the majority of ..."@en .

pg:Q1_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A Bitcoin transaction has no field called fee; the fee is whatever is left over when the values of all the outputs are subtracted from the values of all the inputs, and the miner who includes the transaction collects that ..."@en .

pg:Q2_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Since segregated witness, the size of a transaction that matters for fees is its weight, in weight units: the bytes without witness data count four times and the witness bytes (the signatures) once, so weight is three times ..."@en .

pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "A block may weigh at most 4,000,000 weight units, a limit defined by BIP 141 and written in Bitcoin Core as the constant MAX_BLOCK_WEIGHT, together with the witness scale factor of 4 (Lombrozo et al., 2015)."@en .

pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Because fees are bids in an auction, no one can know in advance which fee rate will be enough, but a full node can learn from the past: it notes when it first saw each transaction, in which block it was confirmed and what fee ..."@en .

pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "The RPC takes a confirmation target in blocks from 1 to 1008, as its help text in the source says, but a target is only as good as the history behind it: the estimator keeps several time scales and the function ..."@en .

pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Replace by fee (RBF) means that a node replaces a transaction in its mempool with a conflicting one that pays more."@en .

pg:Q7_O3 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "If every wallet signalled, the signal would say nothing; the chapter therefore counted how many real transactions signal."@en .

pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "When a child spends an output of an unconfirmed parent, a miner who wants the child must also take the parent, so the sensible thing to price is the pair: the book calls it a package, with an aggregate size and aggregate fees, ..."@en .

pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ;
    rdfs:label "Child pays for parent works because miners rank packages and not single transactions; since release 0.13.0, Bitcoin Core builds its block templates from the feerate of a transaction inclusive of its unconfirmed ancestors ..."@en .

pg:S_AbsoluteFeeRule a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Absolute fee rule"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AbsoluteFeeRule" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_AbsoluteFeeRule> ;
    skos:definition "A replacement must pay at least the absolute fee of what it replaces: a replacement with 514 satoshis was refused with less fees than conflicting txs, and one with the same 564 satoshis with not enough additional fees to relay."@en .

pg:S_AbsurdFeeGuard a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Absurd fee guard"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AbsurdFeeGuard" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_AbsurdFeeGuard> ;
    skos:definition "The default guard of sendrawtransaction is COIN / 10 per kilo-virtual-byte, 10,000 satoshis per virtual byte; the forgotten-change transaction paid 17,272,727 satoshis per virtual byte and was refused."@en .

pg:S_AncestorFeerateMining a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Ancestor feerate mining"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AncestorFeerateMining" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_AncestorFeerateMining> ;
    skos:definition "Since release 0.13.0 miners rank transactions with their unconfirmed ancestors; in the 31.1 node the parent's chunk fee equals the child's ancestor fee, and both chunks weigh 1,122 weight units."@en .

pg:S_AnchorOutputs a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Anchor outputs"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AnchorOutputs" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_AnchorOutputs> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a transaction that is signed long before it is sent can still be fee bumped: with an output that anyone can spend, the pay-to-anchor script, and the allowance of one dust output without fee (Sanders, 2025)."@en .

pg:S_AnchorSpend a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Anchor spend"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#AnchorSpend" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_AnchorSpend> ;
    skos:definition "The child spent the anchor with an empty witness stack, so anyone could have attached it, and paid 10,000 satoshis from a 1-bitcoin input."@en .

pg:S_BlockFeeCensus a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block fee census"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockFeeCensus" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_BlockFeeCensus> ;
    skos:definition "Of the first 599 transactions after the coinbase, the median fee rate was 1,436 satoshis per virtual byte in block 840,000 (2024-04-20) and 3.02 in block 971,489 (2026-10-15)."@en .

pg:S_BlockRewardMix a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block reward mix"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockRewardMix" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_BlockRewardMix> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what part of a miner's income are fees: the subsidy and its halvings, the fee share of two real blocks and the arguments about a future of fees only (Nakamoto, 2008)."@en .

pg:S_BlockWeightLimit a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Block weight limit"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#BlockWeightLimit" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_BlockWeightLimit> ;
    skos:definition "A block may weigh at most 4,000,000 weight units (the constant MAX_BLOCK_WEIGHT); block 840,000 weighed 3,993,281, which is 998,321 virtual bytes and 99.83 percent of the limit."@en .

pg:S_CarveOut a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "CPFP carve out"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CarveOut" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_CarveOut> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what the carve-out was: a 2018 exception to the descendant limits for Lightning, its real size, the quadratic reasoning behind the limits and its removal in release 31.0 (Corallo, 2018)."@en .

pg:S_CarveOutRemoved a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Carve out removed"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CarveOutRemoved" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_CarveOutRemoved> ;
    skos:definition "The carve-out was removed from the mempool in release 31.0 because the cluster limits replaced the descendant limits it worked around."@en .

pg:S_CarveOutRule a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Carve out rule"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CarveOutRule" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_CarveOutRule> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core's carve-out admitted one extra child of at most 10,000 virtual bytes beyond the 25-descendant and 101,000-byte limits (the constant is EXTRA_DESCENDANT_TX_SIZE_LIMIT of 10000), where the book says 1,000 and 100,000; Corallo's 2018 proposal had said 1K."@en .

pg:S_ChildBeatsHighRateTransaction a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Child beats high rate transaction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChildBeatsHighRateTransaction" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ChildBeatsHighRateTransaction> ;
    skos:definition "In a block with room for two of the three transactions, the node chose the parent and its child (package rate 50.8) before an unrelated transaction paying 20 satoshis per virtual byte, and the parent comes before the child in the block."@en .

pg:S_ChildPaysForParent a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Child pays for parent (CPFP)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChildPaysForParent" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ChildPaysForParent> ;
    skos:definition "The fifth and sixth sections of the chapter are about the second fee bumping method, child pays for parent, and about package relay that makes it work when the parent is too cheap to be kept (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_ChunksAndLinearization a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Chunks and linearization"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ChunksAndLinearization" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ChunksAndLinearization> ;
    skos:definition "A cluster is linearized (put in an order that keeps parents before children) so that the highest fee rate prefix, a chunk, comes first; the parent and child of the run formed one chunk of 1,122 weight units."@en .

pg:S_ClusterLimits a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Cluster limits"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ClusterLimits" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ClusterLimits> ;
    skos:definition "Release 31.0 limits a cluster (a connected set of mempool transactions) to 64 transactions and 101,000 virtual bytes; a chain of 65 payments was accepted up to 64 and the 65th refused with too-large-cluster."@en .

pg:S_ClusterMempool a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Cluster mempool"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ClusterMempool" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ClusterMempool> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how the mempool is organised in release 31.0: in clusters of at most 64 transactions, ordered into chunks, replacing the old limits of 25 related transactions (Bitcoin Core developers, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_CoinbaseIsExcluded a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Coinbase is excluded"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#CoinbaseIsExcluded" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_CoinbaseIsExcluded> ;
    skos:definition "The first transaction of block 840,000 spends no earlier output (it is the coinbase), and the block's reward of 4,075,061,499 satoshis equals the subsidy of 312,500,000 plus the 3,762,561,499 satoshis of fees."@en .

pg:S_ConfirmationTargetLimits a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Confirmation target limits"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ConfirmationTargetLimits" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ConfirmationTargetLimits> ;
    skos:definition "The RPC accepts targets from 1 to 1008 blocks, but the estimator can only look back as far as its own history allows, so on a chain 231 blocks high the target 144 was answered with an estimate for 60 blocks."@en .

pg:S_ConflictRule a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Conflict rule"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ConflictRule" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ConflictRule> ;
    skos:definition "Two transactions conflict when they spend a common output: version 1 (inputs A and B) conflicts with version 0 (input A) and replaced it in the mempool, and version 2 (inputs B and C) conflicts with version 1, but not with version 0."@en .

pg:S_DefaultModeEconomical a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Default mode economical"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DefaultModeEconomical" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_DefaultModeEconomical> ;
    skos:definition "Since release 28.0 the default mode of estimatesmartfee is economical, not conservative: on the 31.1 node the default answer equals the economical answer at all four targets and differs from the conservative one at 144 blocks."@en .

pg:S_DustThreshold a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Dust threshold"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#DustThreshold" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_DustThreshold> ;
    skos:definition "An output is dust when it costs more to spend than it is worth at 3 satoshis per byte: 294 satoshis for P2WPKH, 330 for P2WSH and 240 for pay-to-anchor; the node refused 293, 329 and 239 and accepted 294, 330 and 240."@en .

pg:S_EphemeralDust a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Ephemeral dust"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EphemeralDust" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_EphemeralDust> ;
    skos:definition "Ephemeral dust allows one dust output in a transaction that pays no fee, provided the child spends it: the zero-fee parent was refused alone and mined with its child, and the mempool was empty after."@en .

pg:S_EstimateSmartFeeCall a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The estimatesmartfee call"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EstimateSmartFeeCall" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_EstimateSmartFeeCall> ;
    skos:definition "A fresh node answers the book's call with Insufficient data or no feerate found; after 120 blocks and 2,640 payments of mixed fee rates it answered 1.225 satoshis per virtual byte for 2 blocks and 1.173 for 6 and 25."@en .

pg:S_EstimatorMechanics a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Estimator mechanics"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EstimatorMechanics" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_EstimatorMechanics> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a node guesses the fee rate that is needed: from the record of the past, with thresholds, targets and a mode that was changed in release 28.0 (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024)."@en .

pg:S_EstimatorThresholds a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Estimator thresholds"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#EstimatorThresholds" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_EstimatorThresholds> ;
    skos:definition "The estimator looks for the lowest fee rate at which more than 85 percent of the transactions were confirmed within the target (60 percent within half of it, 95 percent within double), constants of the source file."@en .

pg:S_FeeAsDifference a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fee as difference"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeAsDifference" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeAsDifference> ;
    skos:definition "The first subject asks what a fee is on the level of the bytes: no field, only a gap between what goes in and what comes out, and the consequences for a sender who forgets a change output (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_FeeBumpWithWallet a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fee bump with wallet"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeBumpWithWallet" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeBumpWithWallet> ;
    skos:definition "The wallet's bumpfee RPC created the replacement itself: a 208-byte transaction at 2 satoshis per virtual byte (416 satoshis, signalling with 0xfffffffd) became one at 7 (1,457 satoshis) and the original left the mempool."@en .

pg:S_FeeEstimation a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fee estimation"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeEstimation" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeEstimation> ;
    skos:definition "The third section shows how a node estimates the fee rate that is needed for a transaction to confirm in a given number of blocks, from its record of the past, and how far the numbers of the real network can be compared (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_FeeFilterMessage a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The feefilter message"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeFilterMessage" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeFilterMessage> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 133 lets a node tell its peers the lowest fee rate it will accept so that they do not announce cheaper transactions, and the book's getpeerinfo listing shows the field minfeefilter of a peer as 0."@en .

pg:S_FeeIsABid a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A fee is a bid"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeIsABid" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeIsABid> ;
    skos:definition "Ten transactions of one size and ten different fees were offered to a node and its block template listed them from the highest fee rate (50 satoshis per virtual byte) to the lowest (1 satoshi per virtual byte)."@en .

pg:S_FeeIsInputsMinusOutputs a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fee is inputs minus outputs"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeIsInputsMinusOutputs" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeIsInputsMinusOutputs> ;
    skos:definition "A transaction of one 20-bitcoin input and one 1-bitcoin output leaves 19 bitcoins (1,900,000,000 satoshis) unspent, and the node lists exactly that as the fee of the transaction in its mempool."@en .

pg:S_FeeMarketOnMainnet a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fee market on mainnet"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeMarketOnMainnet" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeMarketOnMainnet> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what the market looks like in real data: two mainnet blocks 2.5 years apart, a public explorer's recommendation of today and an early study of how people paid fees (Mempool Space, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_FeeNeedsSpendersSignature a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fee needs the spender's signature"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeNeedsSpendersSignature" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeNeedsSpendersSignature> ;
    skos:definition "A signed transaction is accepted by the node; the same transaction with its first output lowered by 5,000 satoshis (a larger fee) is refused because the signature no longer matches."@en .

pg:S_FeeOnlyFuture a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "A future with fees only"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeOnlyFuture" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeOnlyFuture> ;
    skos:definition "Satoshi Nakamoto's paper says transaction fees can fund the incentive once the coins are all in circulation, and a 2016 Princeton paper argues that a fee-only chain is less stable; both passages are found in the saved copies, and neither is recomputed."@en .

pg:S_FeePaidInAnotherTransaction a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fee paid in another transaction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeePaidInAnotherTransaction" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeePaidInAnotherTransaction> ;
    skos:definition "The receiver of an output can pay for the parent in a different transaction: the parent paid 141 satoshis, its child 14,100, and the node mined both together in one block."@en .

pg:S_FeeRateArithmetic a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fee rate arithmetic"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeRateArithmetic" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeRateArithmetic> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how the price of block space is computed and written: the division of the fee by the size, the units in which it is written, the mistakes between units and the guard against absurd amounts (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_FeeRateDefinition a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fee rate definition"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeRateDefinition" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeRateDefinition> ;
    skos:definition "The fee rate is the fee divided by the size: 282 satoshis over 141 virtual bytes is 2.0 satoshis per virtual byte (0.5027 per weight unit), while the book's wording, size divided by weight, would give 0.2513, which is no price at all."@en .

pg:S_FeeSnipingAndIncentives a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fee sniping and incentives"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeeSnipingAndIncentives" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeeSnipingAndIncentives> ;
    skos:definition "The ninth and tenth sections of the chapter are about adding fees correctly and about the defence against fee sniping with the lock time, which only matters when fees are a large part of the miner's income (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_FeerateDiagramRule a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Feerate diagram rule"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeerateDiagramRule" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeerateDiagramRule> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core 31.1 no longer asks only for a higher fee rate; its source requires that a replacement strictly improves the mempool's feerate diagram, the curve of cumulative fee against cumulative size."@en .

pg:S_FeesAndFeeRates a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fees and fee rates"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeesAndFeeRates" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeesAndFeeRates> ;
    skos:definition "The second section teaches that the price of block space is the fee rate, which is the fee divided by the size, and that the size is measured in virtual bytes after the discount for witness data (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_FeesVsSubsidy a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Fees versus subsidy"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FeesVsSubsidy" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FeesVsSubsidy> ;
    skos:definition "Fees were 92.33 percent of the reward in block 840,000 (37.6 bitcoins of fees against 3.125 of subsidy) and 0.46 percent in block 971,489, so the book's remark that the subsidy is much higher than fees is true of some blocks only."@en .

pg:S_FinalSequenceDisablesLockTime a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Final sequence disables lock time"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FinalSequenceDisablesLockTime" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FinalSequenceDisablesLockTime> ;
    skos:definition "A lock time is ignored when every input has the final sequence 0xffffffff, so a lock time of tip plus 1 was accepted with that sequence and refused with 0xfffffffd, and the same transaction became acceptable one block later."@en .

pg:S_ForgottenChangeBecomesFee a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Forgotten change becomes fee"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ForgottenChangeBecomesFee" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ForgottenChangeBecomesFee> ;
    skos:definition "The node refuses the transaction that forgot its change with the reason max-fee-exceeded, and when the sender forces it through, the miner's coinbase pays out the subsidy plus the 19 bitcoins."@en .

pg:S_FullRbfDefault a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Full RBF by default"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#FullRbfDefault" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_FullRbfDefault> ;
    skos:definition "On Bitcoin Core 31.1 a transaction with the final sequence 0xffffffff (no signal) was replaced by a transaction of twice the fee, and getmempoolinfo reports fullrbf true; release 28.0 made this the default."@en .

pg:S_IncrementalFeeRule a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Incremental fee rule"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#IncrementalFeeRule" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_IncrementalFeeRule> ;
    skos:definition "The replacement must also pay the incremental relay fee (0.1 satoshi per virtual byte, 15 satoshis for 141 bytes) on top: 578 satoshis were refused with 14 less than 15, and 579 accepted."@en .

pg:S_LargeChildPin a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Large child pin"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LargeChildPin" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_LargeChildPin> ;
    skos:definition "A payment of 100 virtual bytes with a child of 100,000 makes a replacement pay for 100,100 bytes, 1,001 times the original; in the run a 90,012-byte child raised the price of replacing a 705-satoshi parent to 450,780 satoshis, 639 times."@en .

pg:S_LightningAnchorOutputs a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Lightning anchor outputs"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LightningAnchorOutputs" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_LightningAnchorOutputs> ;
    skos:definition "In the Lightning specification each commitment transaction has two anchor outputs of 330 satoshis, spendable by anyone after 16 blocks, so either side can fee bump by child pays for parent."@en .

pg:S_LockTimeBoundary a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Lock time boundary"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LockTimeBoundary" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_LockTimeBoundary> ;
    skos:definition "A transaction is final in a block when its lock time is below the block's height, so a lock time equal to the height is not: on the node a lock time of the next block's height was refused as non-final and rejected from a block, and the tip's height was accepted."@en .

pg:S_LockTimeDefense a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Lock time defense against fee sniping"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LockTimeDefense" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_LockTimeDefense> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a wallet defends against fee sniping with the lock time: the rule of Bitcoin Core, its fallback, the use on the real chain, the exact boundary of the consensus rule, the effect of the final sequence and the proposal for taproot (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_LowFeeChildLoses a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Low fee child loses"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#LowFeeChildLoses" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_LowFeeChildLoses> ;
    skos:definition "With a child paying only 5 satoshis per virtual byte the package rate is 3.0, below the 20 of the unrelated transaction, which was mined alone while the parent and child stayed in the mempool."@en .

pg:S_MainnetPrevHeightShare a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Share of mainnet transactions with the previous height"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MainnetPrevHeightShare" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_MainnetPrevHeightShare> ;
    skos:definition "On mainnet 89 of the first 599 transactions of block 840,000 (15 percent) and 33 of block 971,489 (6 percent) carry the previous block's height as lock time, and 507 and 563 carry 0."@en .

pg:S_MempoolSizeAndExpiry a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Mempool size and expiry"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MempoolSizeAndExpiry" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_MempoolSizeAndExpiry> ;
    skos:definition "The default mempool holds 300 megabytes (-maxmempool) and forgets transactions after 336 hours, which is 14 days; the book's summary 'about one day's worth' describes neither number."@en .

pg:S_MinRelayFee a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Minimum relay fee"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MinRelayFee" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_MinRelayFee> ;
    skos:definition "At the default minimum relay fee of 0.1 satoshi per virtual byte a 141-byte transaction needs 15 satoshis: the node refused 8 and 14 with min relay fee not met, and accepted 15."@en .

pg:S_MinerChoice a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Miner choice"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MinerChoice" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_MinerChoice> ;
    skos:definition "The third subject asks who decides which transactions enter a block: the miner, who treats the fees as bids and fills a scarce space with the highest-paying transactions, and who alone may claim the subsidy in the coinbase (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_MinimumRates a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Minimum rates"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MinimumRates" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_MinimumRates> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how low a fee rate may be: the minimum that nodes relay, the minimum that the wallet accepts and the smallest output that is worth creating, the dust limit (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_MinimumRelayProblem a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The minimum relay problem"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#MinimumRelayProblem" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_MinimumRelayProblem> ;
    skos:definition "A parent paying 8 satoshis (below the 15 needed) was refused alone with min relay fee not met, a child alone was refused as spending a missing output, and the two together were accepted by submitpackage."@en .

pg:S_NoFeeFieldInTransaction a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "No fee field in transaction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#NoFeeFieldInTransaction" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_NoFeeFieldInTransaction> ;
    skos:definition "Parsing the raw bytes of a transaction with an independent parser gives version, inputs, outputs, witness and lock time and no field called fee: the fee exists only as a difference."@en .

pg:S_OneParentOneChild a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "One parent one child"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OneParentOneChild" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_OneParentOneChild> ;
    skos:definition "Release 28.0 pairs a transaction whose fee rate is too low with its child and submits them as one package, and release 31.0 lets the parent of such a one-parent-one-child package pay nothing even when it is not version 3."@en .

pg:S_OptInSignalRule a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Opt-in signal rule (BIP 125)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#OptInSignalRule" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_OptInSignalRule> ;
    skos:definition "A transaction signals replaceability when at least one input has a sequence number below 0xfffffffe: 0xfffffffd signals, 0xfffffffe and 0xffffffff do not, and every transaction of Bitcoin Core's wallet in the run used 0xfffffffd."@en .

pg:S_PackageFeeRate a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Package fee rate"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PackageFeeRate" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_PackageFeeRate> ;
    skos:definition "A parent paying 141 satoshis and a child paying 14,100 form a package of 14,241 satoshis over 280.5 virtual bytes (1,122 weight units), 50.77 satoshis per virtual byte, the figure the node's chunk fields show, or 50.5 when each size is rounded up to 141."@en .

pg:S_PackageRelay a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Package relay"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PackageRelay" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_PackageRelay> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a parent that is too cheap to be relayed can be helped by its child: by sending them together, with the RPC submitpackage, the relay of one parent and one child, and the message with which a peer names its minimum (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024)."@en .

pg:S_PackagesAndMining a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Packages and mining"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PackagesAndMining" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_PackagesAndMining> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how a miner prices a transaction together with its unconfirmed ancestors, the idea behind child pays for parent, and when a child helps and when it does not (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_PayToAnchor a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Pay to anchor (P2A)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PayToAnchor" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_PayToAnchor> ;
    skos:definition "The pay-to-anchor output is the witness version 1 program 0x4e73, the script 51024e73, and anyone can spend it; the run's anchor output carried exactly that script and the value 0."@en .

pg:S_PinningAndAnchors a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Pinning and anchors"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#PinningAndAnchors" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_PinningAndAnchors> ;
    skos:definition "The seventh and eighth sections of the chapter describe transaction pinning, in which the rules that protect nodes also make fee bumping impossible, and the carve-out and anchor outputs, which were made for Lightning (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_QuadraticWork a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Quadratic work"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#QuadraticWork" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_QuadraticWork> ;
    skos:definition "If a child pays for 25 ancestors that each have 25 other children, about 625 packages must be updated, and doubling the limit to 50 quadruples the work to 2,500, the cost that the old limits guarded against."@en .

pg:S_RecommendedFeesToday a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Recommended fees today"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RecommendedFeesToday" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_RecommendedFeesToday> ;
    skos:definition "On 2026-10-15 the public explorer recommended 1 satoshi per virtual byte for every speed, and its series of 1,096 block-group averages over the last year ran from 798,426 to 8,098,275 satoshis of fees per block."@en .

pg:S_RemovedAncestorLimits a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Removed ancestor limits"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RemovedAncestorLimits" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_RemovedAncestorLimits> ;
    skos:definition "The old limits of 25 ancestors or descendants and 101,000 virtual bytes are gone in release 31.0: a chain whose 26th transaction the old rule would have refused was accepted, and the 64th as well."@en .

pg:S_RemovedOption a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Removed option"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RemovedOption" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_RemovedOption> ;
    skos:definition "Release 29.0 removed the option -mempoolfullrbf, and a 31.1 node started with -mempoolfullrbf=0 stops with Invalid parameter -mempoolfullrbf=0 and exit code 1."@en .

pg:S_ReplaceByFee a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Replace by fee (RBF)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ReplaceByFee" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ReplaceByFee> ;
    skos:definition "The fourth section is about replacing a transaction by a conflicting one that pays more, the first of the two fee bumping methods, and it is the part of the chapter that has changed most: full replacement is now the default and its option has been removed (Bitcoin Core developers, 2024) (Bitcoin Core developers, 2025)."@en .

pg:S_ReplaceableSignal a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Replaceable signal"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ReplaceableSignal" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ReplaceableSignal> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks which transactions can be replaced: those that signal, as BIP 125 defined, or all, which is now the default since release 28.0, and the option that was removed in release 29.0 (Harding, 2015) (Bitcoin Core developers, 2025)."@en .

pg:S_ReplacementCycling a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Replacement cycling"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ReplacementCycling" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ReplacementCycling> ;
    skos:definition "Replacement cycling lets a counterparty repeatedly replace and remove a victim's time-sensitive transaction so that it never confirms in time; the claim is the Optech text, found in the saved copies, and the attack itself was not executed."@en .

pg:S_ReplacementHazards a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Replacement hazards"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ReplacementHazards" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ReplacementHazards> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what can go wrong with replacement: a payee paid twice, a replacement made expensive by a big child, and an attack that cycles replacements (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_ReplacementRules a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Replacement rules"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ReplacementRules" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ReplacementRules> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks what a replacement must pay: the absolute fee, the incremental fee, and the idea of the feerate diagram, together with the wallet command that does it all (Harding, 2015) (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_RevealedFeeBehaviour a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Revealed fee behaviour"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#RevealedFeeBehaviour" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_RevealedFeeBehaviour> ;
    skos:definition "A study of June 2012 to May 2013 found a median wait of 1,339 seconds for transactions with no fee, 600 for those paying 0.0005 bitcoin and 520 for 0.001, the first empirical look at fee behaviour; the claim is the table of the paper, found in the saved copy."@en .

pg:S_SignalInTheWild a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Signal in the wild"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SignalInTheWild" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_SignalInTheWild> ;
    skos:definition "In the first 599 transactions after the coinbase, 485 signalled replaceability in block 840,000 (81 percent) and 273 in block 971,489 (46 percent), so signalling is common but no longer needed."@en .

pg:S_SizeAndWeight a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Size and weight"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SizeAndWeight" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_SizeAndWeight> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks in what unit the size of a transaction is measured, because a price per unit needs a unit: weight units and virtual bytes, with the discount for witness data and the limit of the block (Lombrozo et al., 2015)."@en .

pg:S_SnipingAttack a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Sniping attack"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SnipingAttack" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_SnipingAttack> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how likely and how dangerous fee sniping is: the formula of its chance and the undercutting attack of the Princeton study (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)."@en .

pg:S_SnipingProbability a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Sniping probability"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SnipingProbability" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_SnipingProbability> ;
    skos:definition "A miner with a share x of the hash rate wins the race to re-mine the last block and extend it with probability (x / (1 - x)) squared: 1.23 percent for 10 percent, 18.37 percent for 30 percent and 66.94 percent for 45 percent, reaching certainty at one half."@en .

pg:S_SpenderAndSignature a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Spender and signature"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SpenderAndSignature" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_SpenderAndSignature> ;
    skos:definition "The second subject asks who can pay a fee: the sender, because the signature covers the outputs, and the receiver only through a second transaction (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_SubmitPackageRpc a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "The submitpackage RPC"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SubmitPackageRpc" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_SubmitPackageRpc> ;
    skos:definition "The RPC submitpackage accepted a version-3 parent paying nothing together with a child paying 10,000 satoshis for both, an effective rate of 36.5 satoshis per virtual byte over 274 virtual bytes."@en .

pg:S_SubsidyHalvings a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Subsidy halvings"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#SubsidyHalvings" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_SubsidyHalvings> ;
    skos:definition "The subsidy is 50 bitcoins shifted right once per 210,000 blocks (GetBlockSubsidy): 625,000,000 satoshis before block 840,000 and 312,500,000 from it, as in block 971,489."@en .

pg:S_TaprootAntiSniping a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Taproot anti-fee-sniping (BIP 326)"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TaprootAntiSniping" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_TaprootAntiSniping> ;
    skos:definition "BIP 326 proposes that taproot wallets use the sequence field instead of the lock time against fee sniping, so that the lock time does not mark some transactions as unusual; the passage is found in the saved copy and the proposal is not executed."@en .

pg:S_ThreeVersionsPaidTwice a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Three versions paid twice"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#ThreeVersionsPaidTwice" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_ThreeVersionsPaidTwice> ;
    skos:definition "When version 0 (input A), version 1 (A and B) and version 2 (B and C) of one payment of 0.4 bitcoin were made, a miner that kept version 0 mined it and then version 2, and the payee received 0.8 bitcoin."@en .

pg:S_TrucLimits a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "TRUC limits"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TrucLimits" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_TrucLimits> ;
    skos:definition "A version-3 (TRUC) transaction may have one unconfirmed parent and one child, at most 10,000 virtual bytes itself and 1,000 for the child; a child of 1,350 virtual bytes was refused, a child of 141 accepted."@en .

pg:S_TrucPinningBound a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "TRUC pinning bound"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TrucPinningBound" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_TrucPinningBound> ;
    skos:definition "With a version-3 child capped at 1,000 virtual bytes (885 in the run) the same replacement cost 5,145 satoshis, 88 times less than the 450,780 of the unrestricted pin."@en .

pg:S_TrucPolicy a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "TRUC policy"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TrucPolicy" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 2 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_TrucPolicy> ;
    skos:definition "This subject asks how version-3 transactions solve the problems of pinning: one parent, one child, small sizes, sibling eviction and a bound on the cost of a replacement (Zhao, 2024)."@en .

pg:S_TrucSiblingEviction a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "TRUC sibling eviction"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TrucSiblingEviction" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_TrucSiblingEviction> ;
    skos:definition "A second child of the same parent paying the same rate was refused for insufficient fee including sibling eviction, and one paying a higher rate was accepted and evicted the first child."@en .

pg:S_TrucVersionMixing a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "TRUC version mixing"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#TrucVersionMixing" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_TrucVersionMixing> ;
    skos:definition "A version-2 transaction that spends an output of an unconfirmed version-3 transaction is refused with TRUC-violation, because the rules bind both sides."@en .

pg:S_UndercuttingAttack a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Undercutting attack"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#UndercuttingAttack" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_UndercuttingAttack> ;
    skos:definition "Carlsten and his coauthors show that with only fees miners profit by forking the head of the chain and leaving out its transactions, which they call undercutting; the passage is found in the saved copy and the attack is not recomputed."@en .

pg:S_UnitsOfFeeRate a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Units of fee rate"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#UnitsOfFeeRate" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_UnitsOfFeeRate> ;
    skos:definition "The book's estimatesmartfee answer 0.00006570 bitcoin per kilo-virtual-byte is exactly 6.57 satoshis per virtual byte, and the 31.1 node's own answer 0.00001225 is 1.225, and 1 bitcoin per kilo-virtual-byte would be 100,000 satoshis per virtual byte."@en .

pg:S_WalletLagFallback a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Wallet lag fallback"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WalletLagFallback" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_WalletLagFallback> ;
    skos:definition "When the node is still syncing or its tip is older than 8 hours the wallet sets the lock time to 0, so as not to leak a unique fingerprint."@en .

pg:S_WalletLockTimeRule a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Wallet lock time rule"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WalletLockTimeRule" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_WalletLockTimeRule> ;
    skos:definition "The wallet sets the lock time to the height of the tip, and in about one case in ten back-dates it by a random 0 to 99 blocks: 56 of 60 transactions in the run had the tip, four were back-dated by 6, 28, 35 and 54."@en .

pg:S_WalletMinimumFee a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Wallet minimum fee"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WalletMinimumFee" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_WalletMinimumFee> ;
    skos:definition "Bitcoin Core's wallet has its own minimum of 1 satoshi per virtual byte (DEFAULT_TRANSACTION_MINFEE of 1000 per kilo-virtual-byte), ten times the 0.1 that nodes relay, and refused 0.5 and 0.1 while accepting 1."@en .

pg:S_WeightAndVirtualSize a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Weight and virtual size"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WeightAndVirtualSize" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_WeightAndVirtualSize> ;
    skos:definition "A one-input, two-output P2WPKH transaction has 113 bytes without witness and 222 with it, so its weight is 3 x 113 + 222 = 561 weight units and its virtual size is 561 / 4 rounded up, 141 virtual bytes, as the node says."@en .

pg:S_WhoPaysTheFee a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Who pays the fee"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WhoPaysTheFee" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 1 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_WhoPaysTheFee> ;
    skos:definition "The first section of the chapter explains that a transaction fee is a bid in an auction for the room in a block, that it is paid by the spender because only the spender's signature can commit to it, and that it is nothing but the gap between inputs and outputs (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."@en .

pg:S_WrongUnitFactor a ipo:ChapterSection,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Wrong unit factor"@en ;
    sen0401:anchor "#WrongUnitFactor" ;
    sen0401:outlineLevel 3 ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09#S_WrongUnitFactor> ;
    skos:definition "The wallet accepts fee_rate in satoshis per virtual byte and feeRate in bitcoin per kilo-virtual-byte: 10 and 0.0001 give the identical fee of 1,410 satoshis, while the number 10 in the second field is refused."@en .

pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Show that a fee is the gap between what a transaction's inputs hold and its outputs hold, say why only the spender's signature can commit to it, and describe what a miner chooses"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can show that a fee is the gap between what a transaction's inputs hold and its outputs hold, say why only the spender's signature can commit to it, and describe what a miner chooses."@en .

pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Compute the virtual size and the fee rate of a transaction from its weight, convert between the units a node uses, and state the minimum rates and the dust limits a node applies"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can compute the virtual size and the fee rate of a transaction from its weight, convert between the units a node uses, and state the minimum rates and the dust limits a node applies."@en .

pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain how a node estimates the fee rate for a target number of blocks from the past, what it needs before it answers, and read the fee market of two mainnet blocks"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain how a node estimates the fee rate for a target number of blocks from the past, what it needs before it answers, and read the fee market of two mainnet blocks."@en .

pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "State what a replacement transaction must pay, how the opt-in signal differs from full replacement, and judge the hazards that replacement brings"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can state what a replacement transaction must pay, how the opt-in signal differs from full replacement, and judge the hazards that replacement brings."@en .

pg:CO6 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Explain fee sniping and the lock time defence, and judge how much the defence matters when fees are a small or a large part of a miner's income"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Evaluate ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can explain fee sniping and the lock time defence, and judge how much the defence matters when fees are a small or a large part of a miner's income."@en .

pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective,
        owl:NamedIndividual ;
    rdfs:label "Work out the package fee rate that lifts a cheap parent, explain package relay and the cluster mempool, and say how a large child pins a parent and what version 3 and anchors change"@en ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze ;
    dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0401/ch09/document> ;
    skos:definition "After this chapter the learner can work out the package fee rate that lifts a cheap parent, explain package relay and the cluster mempool, and say how a large child pins a parent and what version 3 and anchors change."@en .

